{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Structured Data Types", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 9, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear and use when talking about structured data types.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Pronouns One and Ones", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use <strong>one</strong> for singular and <strong>ones</strong> for plural. Using these pronouns, we avoid repeating a noun that was mentioned before. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "I could be the one who helps you.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We use <strong>one</strong> if we talk about a singular noun, for example:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "\t<center><span style=\"font-style:italic;\">A: Could you give me <strong>the book </strong> about structured data types?</br>  B: Yes, the <strong>one</strong> the teacher had? </br> \tA: Did you see <strong>the teacher</strong>? </br>   B: Which <strong>one</strong>? The <strong>tall one</strong>?</div> ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "We use <strong>ones</strong> if we want to avoid repeating a plural noun, take a look at the following examples:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<center><span style=\"font-style:italic;\">A: Did you see <strong>my notes </strong>?</br>  B: Which <strong>ones</strong>? The <strong>ones</strong> about Pascal? </br> ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Considering the fact that we already know what we are talking about, we use <strong>one</strong> or <strong>ones</strong> in order to avoid unnecessary repetitions.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "That one an hold a collection of variables or values.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "That one  is used for collecting different data types that define a single thing.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "(\u2026) the ones who are new in this programming language tend to forget the end keyword.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Noun Clauses", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Think of a <strong>noun clause</strong> as a group of words that work together as a single noun, which can be a subject, object or complement. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You know that the data types that we've studied so far can take just one value, right?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "A <strong>noun clause</strong> is called<strong> dependent or subordinate clause</strong>. It contains a subject and a verb but does not express a whole idea. As a result, it is only a fragment that can't stand by itself. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<strong>Noun clauses</strong> begin with words such as:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<center>How, that, what, whatever, when, where, whether, which, who and why.</div>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">For example:</br>\u2022\tOnce we understand this topic, we can do <strong>whatever we want</strong>.</br>\u2022\tWe have to know <strong>how this works</strong>.</br>\u2022\tDo you know <strong>what the next topic will be</strong>?</br>\u2022\tI'm reading <strong>that structured data types are easy!</strong></br>\u2022\tOur teacher told me <strong>that today's lesson is cancelled</strong>.</br>\u2022\tI like <strong>what I read</strong>. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "That one is used for collecting different data types that define a single thing.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Grammar Concerns: Adjectives vs. Adverbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "When learning a language, it's important to know the difference between<b> adjectives </b> and <b>adverbs</b>. They are both used to describe, but you have to know when to use each. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Are you ready for today's test?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Think of <strong>adjectives</strong> as the words in charge of giving a characteristic to a noun or pronoun, for example:", "type": "text"}, {"content": " <span style=\"font-style:italic;\">\u2022\tAn <strong>interesting</strong> book</br>\u2022\tA <strong>long</strong> and <strong>boring</strong> lesson</br>\u2022\tShe is a <strong>hardworking</strong> student.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span style=\"font-style:italic;\"><strong>Interesting, long, boring </strong> and <strong>hardworking </strong>are <strong>adjectives</strong> used to describe nouns (a book, a lesson or a woman). ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Now,<strong> adverbs</strong> are used to describe actions or verbs. Let's consider the following sentences:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">\u2022\tShe works <strong>patiently.</strong></br> \u2022\tYou need to finish your assignment <strong>quickly</strong>.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Notice that <strong>patiently</strong> does not refer to the woman performing the action (to work), but to the action itself. The same thing happens in the second sentence because the adverb <strong>quickly</strong> refers to the verb finish. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Take into account the following list of some basic adjectives and adverbs that have the same meaning:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong><center>Adjectives</div></strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong><center>Adverbs</div></strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Good\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Well\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Bad\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Badly*\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Perfect\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Perfectly*\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Safe\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Safely*\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Hard**</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Hard</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Loud\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Loudly*\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Immediate\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Immediately*\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "*Notice you can write an adverb using an adjective as a root and adding the suffix \u2013<strong>ly</srong>t at the end. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": " **There are some words like <strong>hard, late, clear or fast</strong> that are both adjectives and adverbs. Be careful with these exceptions.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "See? You're clever.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I've been studying eagerly.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Structured data types are common in modern computer languages.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We can say then that structured data types help define complex data structures", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We needed to write the code carefully.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "pass ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to have a good result in a test or exam", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I must pass it. I don't feel ready.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "piece of data ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a small part of information", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A type can define the meaning, the usage and the processes for storing the different values within a piece of data. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "eagerly ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "it's used for describing when something is done with enthusiasm.", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adverb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I've been studying eagerly.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "programmer ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a person who writes code for computer programs or devices", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It's a classification that programmers do according to the use that they want to give to the data.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "feel like a million dollars ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to feel extremely happy or good", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I would feel like a million dollars if I pass the exam.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "bookworm", "type": "name"}, {"content": "someone who studies or reads a lot", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You're a bookworm! ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob09aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Look for more information of examples related to structured data types.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a paragraph in which you describe each example and the reason why they are structured data types. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Be careful with spelling and grammar. Make room for the grammatical topics of the lesson (adjectives vs. adverbs, noun clauses and pronouns one and ones).", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your paragraph with a partner.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I look for more examples of structured data types.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a paragraph including and describing those examples.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I try to include the grammar topics of the lesson and check spelling.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I read my paragraph to a partner. ", "type": "text"}]}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can describe structured data types.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can give detailed information related to structured data types.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about key features related to structured data types.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What do you know about modern programming languages?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Which data types do you know?", "type": "Free